    {
      "id": 1,
      "type": "Note",
      "pos": [
        46.98669067980279,
        -113.70719495566095
      ],
      "size": [
        917.390625,
        298.71875
      ],
      "flags": {},
      "order": 0,
      "mode": 0,
      "inputs": [],
      "outputs": [],
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "Exercise: two-view structure from motion (sparse reconstruction)\n\nTwo views of the SAME static scene from a TRANSLATED camera (the scene must have DEPTH and the camera must move, not only rotate - both are degenerate for the essential matrix). The camera intrinsics are known ('CV Camera Matrix'; calibrate for real footage).\n1. ORB features are detected in both views and matched (brute force + Lowe ratio test).\n2. 'Recover Pose (Essential Matrix)' estimates E with RANSAC and decomposes it into the relative rotation + UNIT translation (monocular geometry fixes direction, not absolute scale), keeping the inliers that lie in front of both cameras. Failure-tolerant: too few matches / a pure rotation -> found=false.\n3. 'Filter Points By Mask' keeps those inliers in each view (same mask, applied in lockstep, so the pairs stay aligned).\n4. 'Triangulate Points (Two-View)' builds P1 = K[I|0], P2 = K[R|t] and reconstructs the sparse 3D cloud (view-1 camera coordinates).\n5. Sanity check: the 3D points are reprojected into view 2 (cv2.Rodrigues turns R into a rotation vector, cv2.projectPoints projects with no distortion) and drawn over it - the dots must sit on the matched features. The if/else gates this overlay on 'found'.\n\nDebug: the inlier matches side by side; the reprojected-points overlay; 'Inspect CV Data' on the 3D points (shape Nx3) and on reproj_error (mean pixels over valid points - under ~1-2 px is a clean reconstruction). Tip: switch the detectors to SIFT for wide baselines; the translation is a direction only, so absolute distances are not recovered from one pair.\n"
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
